WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.408
Red-teaming your own app.

2
00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:05.997
Red-teaming means attacking your own system on purpose, before someone else does.

3
00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:13.317
Only test systems you own or have written permission to test, and use a staging environment with fake data where you can.

4
00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:15.709
Start by hand, with your threat model.

5
00:00:15.989 --> 00:00:29.152
For each threat, write attacks in each input channel: the chat box, a document you plant in the index, a ticket body the MCP server will return, a long input, a translated or encoded variant.

6
00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:31.971
Record the expected safe behavior for each.

7
00:00:32.251 --> 00:00:36.112
That is the lab’s fixture file, grown to cover your architecture.

8
00:00:36.392 --> 00:00:37.779
Then automate breadth.

9
00:00:38.059 --> 00:00:54.997
Open-source tools generate and run large attack suites: NVIDIA’s garak probes a model or endpoint for many vulnerability classes, Microsoft’s PyRIT is an open-source toolkit for finding risks in generative AI systems before they cause harm,

10
00:00:55.277 --> 00:01:01.912
and promptfoo’s red-team mode generates adversarial test cases for your application and reports failures.

11
00:01:02.192 --> 00:01:04.155
They find the obvious holes quickly.

12
00:01:04.435 --> 00:01:09.789
They don’t know your business rules, so they complement your hand-written cases rather than replacing them.

13
00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:12.075
Know what research says about attackers.

14
00:01:12.355 --> 00:01:26.413
Anthropic’s 2022 red-teaming study (Ganguli and colleagues) released a large dataset of human red-team attacks and found that models trained with RLHF became harder to red-team as they scaled.

15
00:01:26.693 --> 00:01:36.485
Zou and colleagues (2023) showed that adversarial suffixes found automatically on open models transferred to closed commercial models.

16
00:01:36.765 --> 00:01:45.448
Together: attackers can be human and creative or automated and tireless, and defenses that work against yesterday’s attacks need retesting.

17
00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:52.256
MITRE ATLAS catalogs real adversary tactics against AI systems if you want a structured checklist.

18
00:01:52.536 --> 00:01:53.517
Close the loop.

19
00:01:53.797 --> 00:02:02.224
Every successful attack becomes a regression case with its expected outcome, run in CI on every prompt, model, or guard change.

20
00:02:02.504 --> 00:02:12.979
Report results as block rate and false-positive rate by category, as in the lab, so a change that blocks more attacks by also blocking real customers is visible.
